WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.790
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:10.270
Style transfer is a technique in machine learning
research where we have two input images, one

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:16.470
for content, and one for style, and the output
is our content image reimagined with this

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:17.470
new style.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:23.260
The cool part is that the content can be a
photo straight from our camera, and the style

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:27.900
can be a painting, which leads to the super
fun results that you see here.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:34.519
An earlier paper had shown that the more sophisticated
ones can sometimes make even art curators

00:00:34.519 --> 00:00:36.300
think that they are real.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.899
This previous work blew me away as it could
perform style transfer for smoke simulations.

00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:46.749
I almost fell out of the chair when I have
first seen these results.

00:00:46.749 --> 00:00:51.480
It could do fire textures, starry night, you
name it.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.510
It seems that it is able to do anything we
can think of.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:58.269
Now let me try to explain two things.

00:00:58.269 --> 00:01:04.850
One, why is this so difficult, and two, the
results are looking really good, so, are there

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:06.490
any shortcomings?

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:11.549
Doing this for smoke simulations is a big
departure from 2D style transfer, because

00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:17.409
that one takes an image, where this works
in 3D, and does not deal with images, but

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:18.570
with density fields.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:24.580
A density field means a collection of numbers
that describe how dense a smoke plume is at

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:25.930
a given spatial position.

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:29.820
It is a physical description of a smoke plume,
if you will.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:36.320
So how could we possibly apply artistic style
from an image to a collection of densities?

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:41.729
The solution in this earlier paper was to
first, downsample the density field to a coarser

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:47.789
version, perform the style transfer there,
and upsample this density field again with

00:01:47.789 --> 00:01:49.810
already existing techniques.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:55.759
This technique was called Transport-based
Neural Style Transfer, TNST in short, please

00:01:55.759 --> 00:01:56.899
remember this.

00:01:56.899 --> 00:02:00.340
Now, let’s look at some results from this
technique.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:05.560
This is what our simulation would look like
normally, and then, all we have to do is show

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:10.440
this image to the simulator, and, what does
it do with it?

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:11.440
Wow.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.740
My goodness.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:15.730
Just look at those heavenly patterns!

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:21.209
So what does today’s new, followup work
offer to us that the previous one doesn’t?

00:02:21.209 --> 00:02:25.330
How can this seemingly nearly perfect technique
be improved?

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:30.360
Well, this new work takes an even more brazen
vantage point to this question.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:36.620
If style transfer on density fields is hard,
then try a different representation.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:41.069
The title of the paper says Lagrangian style
neural style transfer.

00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:42.689
So what does that mean?

00:02:42.689 --> 00:02:44.629
It means particles!

00:02:44.629 --> 00:02:49.330
This was made for particle-based simulations,
which comes with several advantages.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:55.260
One, because the styles are now attached to
particles, we can choose different styles

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:03.140
for different smoke plumes, and they will
remember what style they are supposed to follow.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:07.880
Because of this advantageous property, we
can even ask the particles to change their

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:12.420
styles over time, creating these heavenly
animations.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:19.310
In these 2D examples, you also see how the
texture of the simulation evolves over time,

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:25.830
and that the elements of the style are really
propagated to the surface and the style indeed

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.560
follows how the fluid changes.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.110
This is true, even if we mix these styles
together.

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:39.860
Two, it not only provides us these high-quality
results, but, it is fast.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.540
And by this, I mean blazing fast.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:48.830
You see, we talked about TNST, the transport-based
technique approximately 7 months ago, and

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:54.000
in this series, I always note that two more
papers down the line, and it will be much,

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.410
much faster.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:04:00.720
So here is the Two Minute Papers Moment of
Truth, what do the timings say?

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.730
For the previous technique, it said more than
1d.

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:06.180
What could that 1D mean?

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.290
Oh, goodness!

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:10.790
This thing took an entire day to compute.

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:13.519
So, what about the new one?

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:15.629
What, really?

00:04:15.629 --> 00:04:17.550
Just one hour?

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.000
That is insanity.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:22.920
So, how detailed of a simulation are we talking
about?

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:24.860
Let’s have a look together.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:30.510
M slash f means minutes per frame, and as
you see, if we have tens of thousands of particles,

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:37.190
we have 0.05, or in other words, three seconds
per frame, and we can go up to hundreds of

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:43.670
thousands, or even millions of particles,
and end up around thirty seconds per frame.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:45.240
Loving it.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:48.450
Artists are going to do miracles with this
technique, I am sure.

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:54.150
The next step is likely going to be a real-time
algorithm, which may appear as soon as one

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:58.620
or two more works down the line, and you can
bet your papers that I’ll be here to cover

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:04.050
it, so make sure to subscribe, and hit the
bell icon to not miss it when it appears.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:08.870
The speed of progress in computer graphics
research is nothing short of amazing.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:13.350
Also, make sure to have a look at the full
paper in the video description, not only because

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:19.800
it is a beautiful paper and a lot of fun to
read, but because you will also know what

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:34.680
this regularization step here does exactly

00:05:34.680 --> 00:06:15.350
to the simulation.

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:24.340
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

